    "text": "To complete the `truncate_number` function, you need to subtract the integer part (floor) of the number from the fractional part of it to get just the decimal part. Here's the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\" Given a positive floating point number, it can be decomposed into\n    an integer part (largest integer smaller than given number) and decimals\n    (leftover part always smaller than 1).\n\n    Return the decimal part of the number.\n    >>> truncate_number(3.5)\n    0.5\n    \"\"\"\n    return number - int(number)\n```\n\nThis function works by subtracting the floor value of the integer part from the original number. This gives the integer part of the number, which is then subtracted to get the decimal part.",
